  "narrative_arc": {
    "the_beginning": "It started in the damp woods of the Pacific Northwest. Eli, Marcus, and Sarah weren't looking to build a multi-billion dollar AI company; they were just trying to scare people on Reddit. Their 'Project Hominid' videos were cheap, shaky, and undeniably viral.",
    "the_epiphany": "The breakthrough wasn't algorithmic; it was psychological. 'We noticed the comments,' Sarah recalls, leaning back in her Aeron chair. 'They didn't care if the Sasquatch was a guy in an ape suit. They were arguing over the rustling of the leaves, the specific pixelation of the shadow. They didn't want the truth. They wanted the aesthetic of plausibility.'",
    "the_pivot": "That night, they stopped filming. They realized that producing a single, viral cryptid video was a fool's errand. The real prize was building the ecosystem that generated the rustling leaves, the shadows, the infinite 'what ifs.' They didn't need to find Bigfoot. They needed to own the forest.",
    "the_outcome": "Within six months, they had pivoted from local content creators to data aggregators, buying up the forgotten detritus of analog media—regional weather broadcasts, 1980s corporate training tapes, abandoned B-movie catalogs. They fed the sediment of human culture into a neural network, and out came Sora."
  }
